 "note": "[agent-bulk] Vertex judged from a --vtx-span 60 re-capture (event_half_span_cm 335.8 -- correction 5). Vertex is GOOD: the star (157.4,-41.4,61.8) sits at the apex of a clean V -- 17014 (pdg 13, 289 cm muon) and 17013 (pdg 2212, 17.9 cm proton) both have dvtx 0.0. No vertex complaint. WHY unclear: this event is one shattered on-axis CHAIN and I cannot tell where the chain's true object ends. Shower 87053 (201.7 MeV, 8 seg) = a compact core 14.2 cm from the vertex (cluster 87: 87053 EM + 87055 pdg 211, with the third co-located prong 87054 pdg 13 correctly walk_excluded) plus SIX pass4_angle absorptions from six different clusters strung along +y at 19.5, 43.9, 46.8, 51.7, 62.8 and 67.9 cm (angles 12.9-21.0 deg). One of those, 85037 (17.45 cm, 30 points, at 51.7 cm) is a substantial blob with its own dense point cloud -- on its own it could be read as a SECOND apex, i.e. over-clustering. THE UNDER-CLUSTERING EVIDENCE, which is what I recorded and it is textbook: shw87053-acc shows exactly ONE non-member circle in the whole plot, at 80.6 cm / 12.5 deg, sitting 0.6 cm outside the corner of the 80 cm x 25 deg pass-1 box -- and at a SMALLER angle than four of the six accepted members. That is 66021 (11.7 MeV). Behind it the same chain continues with 71057/71058 at 101.5-102.0 cm / 11.3-11.9 deg (9.3 MeV) and 70025 at 118.6 cm / 10.3 deg (8.8 MeV), both tier 2. So the membership boundary here is set by the 80 cm distance box, not by anything visible in the event: the chain looks identical either side of it. THE TENSION I COULD NOT RESOLVE, stated because it points the other way: the reco pi0 (group 0, 87053+83035) prices at 136.8 MeV on the vertex convention, essentially exactly the pi0 mass. Adding 66021 moves E1 201.7 -> 213.4 and the mass 136.8 -> 140.7; adding all three (29.8 MeV, 15% of the shower) moves it to 146.5. So the geometry says the shower is cut short and the mass says its energy is already right. Two readings: (a) the chain from y=-27 to y=+93 at nearly constant x~157 and z~63-76 is ONE object and the shower is missing its last 30 MeV -- what I recorded; (b) that 120 cm near-vertical chain is a shattered TRACK (a line along +y is close to the collection-wire direction, which is exactly where 3-D reconstruction shatters), the compact core is the real shower, and everything past ~50 cm including 85037 is over-clustering. I cannot separate them from the display. MARK: 66021 IN on 87053 -- this is the WEAK, reversible mark (correction 7): it is the single most defensible piece of the claim geometrically and the piece the pi0 mass argues hardest against. I deliberately did NOT mark 71058 or 70025 in, though they are the same chain at 102 and 119 cm. SECOND SHOWER 83035 (54.5 MeV, 3 seg) is CLEAN: all three segments are cluster 83, from_vertices walk_add, at 0.0/5.6/9.5 cm and 0.0/8.3/4.7 deg, detached 59.7 cm from the vertex -- a textbook converted photon and I have no complaint about it. PI0: the reconstruction's own pairing (87053+83035, stored mass 136.8) is CONFIRMED, not disputed -- in pio mode both gamma arrows point away from the common vertex and the axis convention gives 148.2 vs the vertex convention's 136.8, the usual disagreement pr/114 6.1 documents. NOT looked at: the 9 showers the scope rule skipped, all under 12.7 MeV -- except that four of them (66021, 71058, 70025, and 65020) are the chain objects discussed above, which I did examine through the candidate table and the acceptance plot. drag_check live: probe_radius_px 166, seg_u -92.47 -> 100.38, cloud_redrew true.",
